text:
maria: Heads-up: found a parity gap between node and python SDK retry logic when a token refresh happens during a 429 backoff. Node flings a replay as soon as refresh returns and we see duplicate side-effects in the onboarding-chatbot sampleapp.

aj: Can you paste a minimal repro and logs? Which sampleapp config?

maria: onboarding-chatbot (node). Repro quick: set very low rate limits, short-lived token so refresh overlaps backoff. Logs: 
```
[2026-03-15T12:17:08Z] POST /v1/generate -> 429 Retry-After: 30
[2026-03-15T12:17:08Z] client: starting token refresh
[2026-03-15T12:17:08Z] token refresh: success
[2026-03-15T12:17:08Z] retrying request id=abc123 idempotency-key=msg-789
[2026-03-15T12:17:08Z] POST /v1/generate -> 200 (duplicate resource created)
```

devon: Sounds like the refresh flow is escaping the normal backoff queue. In python we keep retries queued while refresh runs; node marks refresh done and immediately replays with attempt reset so jitter becomes near-zero.

linh: Root cause hypothesis: when refresh completes we recompute delay using attempt=0 or we re-enqueue without honoring the server Retry-After header. Also jitter logic ends up producing tiny delays.

samir: Quick mitigation plan for node SDK: 
- Mark refreshInProgress when refresh starts and prevent immediate replay; keep requests in backoff queue.
- On refresh complete, re-evaluate each pending request's delay = max(serverRetryAfterMs, clientBackoffMs, 50 + random(0,50)).
- Make Idempotency-Key required-by-default for mutating endpoints in sampleapps (and show example in quickstart).

aj: Sketch of change (pseudo):
```js
// on 429
const serverMs = parseRetryAfter(resp.headers['retry-after']) || 0;
enqueueRetry(req, Math.max(serverMs, computeBackoff(attempt)));

// token refresh handler
refreshInProgress = true;
await refreshToken();
refreshInProgress = false;
// do not flush queue immediately; let timers drive retries
```

devon: Also add a small randomized buffer to avoid thundering when refresh ends:
```js
function computeBackoff(attempt){
  const base = 100;
  const exp = Math.min(6, attempt);
  const jitter = Math.random() * (base * Math.pow(2, exp));
  return base * Math.pow(2, exp) + jitter;
}
```

linh: And if server Retry-After is present treat it as a minimum: delay = Math.max(clientDelay, serverRetryMs, 50 + rand(0,50)). That prevents ignoring server guidance.

maria: Action items: 1) patch node SDK in staging, 2) update onboarding sampleapp to send an Idempotency-Key header on mutating calls, 3) add a doc note about auth-refresh interacting with backoff/jitter.

samir: I'll open a small PR with unit tests that simulate token refresh overlapping backoff. Test asserts: no immediate retry after refresh, server Retry-After honored, and idempotency header preserved on retry.

ci-bot: samir/patch/refresh-backoff-queue pushed -> CI started

aj: One more edge: streaming endpoints are tricky because you can't really re-enqueue the same way. For streams we should either fail-fast with a clear error or introduce resumable session IDs that let a client continue after backoff. Add backlog item for streaming semantics.

maria: Good call. Add ticket: "streaming: define resumable semantics for server rate limits / auth refresh" and link sampleapp changes.

samir: PR created: github.com/redwood-inference/node-sdk/pull/642 (includes refreshInProgress flag, respect Retry-After, computeBackoff tweak). Tagging eng-qa for coverage.

maria: After merge please update the quickstart to include an explicit Idempotency-Key example and call out the refresh+backoff gotcha in the "Handling rate limits" section. :thumbsup:

aj: I'll also add a short note to the SDK parity checklist to ensure we have the same semantics across language SDKs.

maria: Thanks everyone. Once staged let's run the onboarding-chatbot stress test and confirm no duplicate side-effects.
